title: "Internal workload on an Odoo project: how many hours?"
lang: "en"
publishedAt: "2026-11-16"
category: "Project Management"
excerpt: "Director, key users, accountant, frontline staff: the hours to free up at each phase of an Odoo project, with published benchmarks, before you sign."
---

Your Odoo partner's quote prices its own hours. It never prices yours. Yet that is where projects go off track.

One observation before going further. Belgian FAQs on duration talk in weeks or months. For example, a first phase covering accounting and invoicing takes 4 to 8 weeks for a company of fewer than 50 people. None of them breaks down the internal workload by role and by phase. Nor do we have a source that gives a complete, reliable table for Belgium.

So this article does something different. It gathers the published figures, credits each one to its source, and gives you a grid to have your partner fill in before you sign. These are orders of magnitude, not commitments.

## The only published total: 200 to 300 hours

A Dutch partner estimates the internal workload of a standard three-month project, in a company with fifteen to twenty-five users, at 200 to 300 hours in total. Over roughly 13 weeks, that works out at around 15 to 23 hours per week for the whole team (that is our division, not theirs).

For comparison: Odoo states that 80% of projects with a standard scope go live in 200 hours or fewer. Those are the partner's hours. Your hours are of the same order of magnitude as theirs.

Another benchmark comes from Odoo's methodology. It allocates 80% of the project to the cycles of analysis, development, validation and key user training, and 5% to go-live (end-user training, fixes). The phases are described differently from our table, but the logic is the same: most of the work is done before launch.

## Who is involved, and when

This matrix is our reading of the sources. It shows the weight of each role in each phase, without putting figures on the hours.

| Role | Scoping | Data migration | Testing | Training | Go-live |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Director | High (decisions) | Medium (decides what to migrate) | Low | Low | Medium (go / no-go) |
| Key users | High | High | High | High | High |
| Accountant | Medium | High (balances, history) | High (test closing) | High | High |
| Frontline team | Low (consulted) | Low | Medium | Medium | High |

## The published figures, role by role

| Role | Published benchmark | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Internal project lead | At least one day per week, more at peak times | Fanatics |
| Internal project lead | At least 30% of their time over the project's duration | Ixemelis |
| Internal project lead | 30 to 50% of their time | Kuantiik |
| Director (sponsor) | Around 10% of their time | TechUltra |
| Key users | 20 to 30% of their time during configuration and testing | Odoo-lab |
| Key user, training | Two to three days in total over the project | Drakkar |
| Accountant (data entry) | 8 to 12 hours of training | Odoocomptaexpert |
| Head of accounting | 16 to 20 hours of training | Odoocomptaexpert |
| End user, training | Half a day to a day on one or two standard modules | Drakkar |
| End user, testing | 5 to 10 hours per user for the acceptance phase | TechUltra |

These sources are partners from different countries and of different sizes. The ranges diverge, notably for the internal project lead (from 30% to 50%). Take the highest figure if your project lead also runs the company.

## Phase by phase: what uses up your hours

**Scoping.** The director and key users make the decisions; the partner documents them. A Belgian provider claims that 80% of projects are won or blown before and around configuration. That is their claim, but it is in line with the other sources. A limited initial scope simplifies scoping, testing, data migration and training. Fewer modules means fewer hours for you.

**Data migration.** This is the phase clients most underestimate. Allow 2 to 4 weeks of cleansing before the import. Disorganised data adds another 2 to 4 weeks. Who decides what to migrate? The accountant for the history, and a designated person for customers, products and stock.

**Testing.** Key users replay their real work, not abstract cases. This is where the 20 to 30% of time is put to the test. If nobody is freed up, acceptance testing is only skimmed and errors appear in production.

**Training.** Key users are trained first, then train their teams. Allow double for custom configurations. Put the sessions in the calendar: otherwise the room is half empty on the day.

**Go-live.** The frontline team is fully involved. Key users absorb the first questions. Plan for availability during the first month, including for the first accounting close.

## What a lack of availability costs

The figures speak for themselves. A British source estimates that the client team's unavailability causes delays of 2 to 8 weeks. Another ranks under-allocation of client time as the most frequent and most easily avoided cause of delay.

Conversely, preparing in advance (cleaned contacts, exported chart of accounts, documented workflows) would save 2 to 4 weeks.

The problem is also one of decision-making. A project without clear governance is a project without a leader. And if two teams each apply their own method, you first have to decide between their practices before configuring anything. That decision falls to the director, not the partner.

## Before you sign: five questions to ask

1. **Name the people.** Not "the accounting department", but one name per role. For each person, list the hours per week and who covers their day-to-day work.

2. **Ask for the schedule of client hours** by role and by phase, alongside the partner's own.
3. **Decide who has the final say** on the data to migrate and the processes to unify.
4. **Check the scope.** Every extra module adds to scoping, testing and training.
5. **Book the testing and training dates** from the start. They are not easy to move.

If your project lead's or key users' hours cannot be freed up, it is better to lengthen the schedule than to shorten the acceptance testing.

